  <p class="secondary">Method paper · 1965</p>
  <h1>New Method for Calculating the One-Particle Green’s Function with Application to the Electron-Gas Problem</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Hedin’s problem is not merely to write an exact many-body equation. It is to reorganize electron–electron perturbation theory around a screened interaction that may remain manageable where an expansion in the bare Coulomb interaction fails. The paper builds that reorganization, tests its first terms on the homogeneous electron gas, and is unusually candid about which quantities converge poorly.
  </p>
  <p class="paper-meta">
    L. Hedin, <cite>Physical Review</cite> 139, A796–A823 (1965). <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.139.A796">DOI 10.1103/PhysRev.139.A796</a>. An open author-deposited scan is indexed by <a href="https://lup.lub.lu.se/search/publication/8777834">Lund University Publications</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The paper asks for a controlled one-particle description</h2>
  <p>
    The starting tension should feel familiar from <a href={href('/core/part-ii/')}>Core Part II</a>. One-particle equations are useful, but an interacting electron does not move only in a fixed external field. Its addition or removal disturbs all the other electrons. Hedin chooses the one-particle Green function because it records exactly the charged sectors relevant to this question: amplitudes and energies for adding one electron to, or removing one electron from, the interacting ground state at fixed nuclear geometry.
  </p>
  <p>
    The paper writes the correction to a Hartree-like propagator as a mass operator <math class="math-inline" aria-label="M"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Modern texts usually call the same object the self-energy <math class="math-inline" aria-label="Sigma"><semantics><mi>Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. In compact operator notation, the equation of motion is
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The inverse interacting Green function equals the inverse Hartree Green function minus the mass operator or self-energy">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo>
          <msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">H</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo>
          <mspace width="0.8em"/><mi>M</mi><mo>↔</mo><mi>Σ</mi><mspace width="0.3em"/><mtext>in modern notation</mtext><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^&#123;-1&#125;=G_&#123;\mathrm&#123;H&#125;&#125;^&#123;-1&#125;-M,\qquad M\leftrightarrow\Sigma\ \text&#123;in modern notation&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Read this as a statement about propagation, not as a new independent-electron Hamiltonian. <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G sub H"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_&#123;\mathrm&#123;H&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> contains kinetic energy, nuclei, and the classical Hartree field. The operator <math class="math-inline" aria-label="M"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> supplies everything that changes the one-particle propagation beyond that reference. Because it can be nonlocal and energy-dependent, it can shift a spectral peak, redistribute its weight, and give it a finite lifetime. Setting it to Hartree–Fock exchange recovers the HF one-particle picture; the exact object is richer.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Hedin, Sec. 2, especially Eqs. (2)–(5), pp. A796–A797. The inverse-propagator line is Atlas operator shorthand for the paper’s coordinate equation.</p>

  <h2>Why screen before expanding?</h2>
  <p>
    A power series in the bare Coulomb interaction <math class="math-inline" aria-label="v"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is a poor organizing principle for a metal: long-range charge rearrangement makes individual terms large and the series can diverge. Hedin’s move is to sum polarization processes into a screened interaction <math class="math-inline" aria-label="W"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> first. An electron then interacts through the potential that remains after the medium has responded, rather than through an unscreened pair potential treated term by term.
  </p>
  <p>
    Appendix A closes the logic with four coupled objects. In modern schematic notation, with repeated composite labels integrated over space, spin, and time, the relations are
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The self-energy, polarization, screened interaction, and vertex form a coupled set of Hedin equations">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.55em" rowspacing="0.7em">
          <mtr><mtd><mi>Σ</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi>ℏ</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>Γ</mi><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><mi>P</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi>ℏ</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>Γ</mi><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><mi>W</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr>
            <mtd><mi>Γ</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd>
            <mtd><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>Σ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi>G</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>Γ</mi><mo>.</mo></mtd>
          </mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\hbar GW\Gamma,\qquad P=-\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\hbar GG\Gamma,\qquad W=v+vPW,\qquad \Gamma=1+\frac&#123;\delta\Sigma&#125;&#123;\delta G&#125;GG\Gamma.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Each line answers a different question. The self-energy says how an electron propagates while interacting with the screened medium. The polarization says how propagating electrons create that screening. The Dyson-like equation for <math class="math-inline" aria-label="W"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> sums repeated polarization of the bare interaction. The vertex <math class="math-inline" aria-label="Gamma"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> corrects the naive coupling between these processes and depends on how the self-energy changes when the propagator changes. Together with the Dyson equation for <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the output of the cycle becomes its next input.
  </p>
  <p>
    The products in this display are not ordinary scalar multiplication. They abbreviate integrations over internal coordinates. That is why the exact closed set is a formal solution strategy, not yet a cheap algorithm: evaluating the vertex requires a functional derivative, and all four objects depend on one another.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Hedin, Appendix A, Eqs. (A20)–(A25), p. A820. The paper uses <em>M</em> for the self-energy and <em>I′</em> for the vertex; the display gives the now-standard schematic translation.</p>

  <figure class="paper-argument hedin-cycle" aria-labelledby="hedin-cycle-caption">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Propagate</strong><span><em>G</em> records one-electron addition and removal</span></li>
      <li><strong>Polarize</strong><span><em>P</em> describes the medium’s charge response</span></li>
      <li><strong>Screen</strong><span><em>W</em> dresses the bare Coulomb interaction</span></li>
      <li><strong>Correct</strong><span><em>Σ</em> changes the one-particle propagation</span></li>
      <li><strong>Close</strong><span>Dyson returns a new <em>G</em>; the vertex couples the cycle consistently</span></li>
    </ol>
    <figcaption id="hedin-cycle-caption">The conceptual cycle behind the exact coupled equations. An approximation chooses which parts of the vertex and self-consistency to retain; solving that chosen system is a separate numerical task.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Read the source diagrams.</strong> In Figs. 1–2 on p. A798, an arrow is <em>G</em> and a wiggly line is <em>W</em>. Start with the first diagram in Fig. 1: one propagator joined by one screened interaction is the first self-energy term. The later diagrams add vertex structure. Fig. 2 performs the parallel expansion for the polarization. These are bookkeeping diagrams for terms in a many-body expansion, not real-space electron trajectories and not measured scattering images.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>The first truncation is what later readers call GW</h2>
  <p>
    The exact cycle becomes a practical approximation only after a choice is made. Hedin first replaces the vertex by its zeroth-order value. The self-energy and polarization then reduce to
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="With the vertex replaced by one, the self-energy is i h bar G W and the polarization is minus i h bar G G">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.55em" rowspacing="0.7em">
          <mtr>
            <mtd><msup><mi>Σ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd>
            <mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi>ℏ</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd>
          </mtr>
          <mtr>
            <mtd><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd>
            <mtd><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi>ℏ</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>)</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd>
          </mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^&#123;GW&#125;(1,2)=\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\hbar G(1,2)W(1^+,2),\qquad P^0(1,2)=-\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\hbar G(1,2^+)G(2,1).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The important approximation is the vertex truncation, not the visual fact that two letters are multiplied. The superscript plus marks an infinitesimally later time: Hedin places it on label 1 in the screened-interaction factor and on label 2 in the first Green-function factor of the polarization, preserving the required equal-time ordering. The bubble polarization <math class="math-inline" aria-label="P zero"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^0</annotation></semantics></math> generates the random-phase screened interaction when inserted into <math class="math-inline" aria-label="W equals v plus v P W"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=v+vPW</annotation></semantics></math>. That screened interaction then enters the first self-energy term. Calling this “GW” is later standard terminology; the 1965 paper describes it as first order in the screened interaction.
  </p>
  <p>
    This distinction also separates theory from solution. In later practice, one can evaluate the same formal GW self-energy once from a chosen input propagator, update only quasiparticle energies, iterate <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, iterate both <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G and W"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mtext> and </mtext><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\ \text&#123;and&#125;\ W</annotation></semantics></math>, or include a vertex. Those are different approximation and self-consistency variants, not mere convergence settings.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Hedin, Appendix A, Eqs. (A27)–(A30), p. A820; compare Sec. 3, Eqs. (10)–(12), p. A798.</p>

  <h2>A static limit turns screening into a physical picture</h2>
  <p>
    Section 4 asks what the first screened-interaction term looks like when the frequency dependence of the important screening processes can be neglected. The result separates into a local Coulomb-hole contribution and a nonlocal screened-exchange contribution:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The COHSEX self-energy is one half the local correlation part of the screened interaction minus nonlocal screened exchange">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>Σ</mi><mtext>COHSEX</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo>
          <mo>[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo>
          <mo>−</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_&#123;\mathrm&#123;COHSEX&#125;&#125;(x,x')=\frac12\delta(x-x')[W(x,x';0)-v(x,x')]-W(x,x';0)\rho_1(x,x').</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The coordinate labels include position and spin; the final factor is the one-particle density matrix connecting two such labels, which is why screened exchange is nonlocal. The first term says that the induced charge surrounding an added electron changes its energy; the factor one half reflects the work needed to build that induced response. The second term resembles Hartree–Fock exchange, but the bare interaction has been replaced by its static screened value. If screening is removed so that <math class="math-inline" aria-label="W equals v"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=v</annotation></semantics></math>, the Coulomb-hole term vanishes and screened exchange returns to ordinary exchange. This limiting case makes the approximation legible.
  </p>
  <p>
    COHSEX is not the general GW self-energy. It discards the frequency dependence that produces dynamical renormalization, lifetimes, and satellite structure. Hedin argues that the static picture can work well for an electron outside a closed shell, but calls it only a rough, reasonable approximation for the metallic electron gas.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Hedin, Sec. 4, Eqs. (18)–(20), p. A799. The paper introduces the abbreviation COHSEX immediately after Eq. (20).</p>

  <h2>The poles become quasiparticle energies</h2>
  <p>
    For the translationally invariant electron gas, momentum labels the Green function. Viewed as a function of energy, the Green function becomes large when its inverse approaches zero. A sufficiently sharp, long-lived addition or removal feature is then identified with a quasiparticle pole, whose energy satisfies
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The quasiparticle energy equals the free-particle energy plus the self-energy evaluated at that quasiparticle energy">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>E</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <mi>ε</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo>
          <mi>Σ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)=\varepsilon(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)+\Sigma(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;,E(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    This is nonlinear even before any global self-consistency loop: the unknown energy appears inside the energy-dependent self-energy. The real part shifts the quasiparticle peak relative to the free or reference dispersion. The imaginary part describes decay and broadening when the pole lies in a continuum. The derivative with respect to energy gives the renormalization factor that controls the pole weight and alters derivatives such as the Fermi-surface velocity.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is a charged addition/removal object. It is not a neutral optical excitation and it is not a claim that a Kohn–Sham eigenvalue is automatically a measured electron energy. <a href={href('/core/part-viii/')}>Core Part VIII</a> keeps those sectors separate.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Hedin, Secs. 2 and 5, Eqs. (4), (5), and (25)–(28), pp. A797–A801.</p>

  <h2>What the electron-gas calculation actually did</h2>
  <p>
    The numerical half of the paper does not solve the exact closed equations. Its physical model is the homogeneous electron gas in a uniform positive background, evaluated mainly for density parameters <math class="math-inline" aria-label="r sub s equals one through six"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_s=1,\ldots,6</annotation></semantics></math>, with a few additional densities. This dimensionless Wigner–Seitz radius is the radius of a sphere containing one electron on average, measured in Bohr radii; larger values mean lower density and stronger interaction relative to the kinetic scale. There is no crystal, ionic pseudopotential, band path, or Brillouin-zone mesh in this model.
  </p>
  <p>
    The decisive implementation choice is visible by comparing what Hedin used with what he says would have been desirable:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The reported calculation uses a shifted free propagator instead of the desired fully self-consistent propagator">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.55em" rowspacing="0.8em">
          <mtr>
            <mtd><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext>used</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd>
            <mtd><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mtd>
          </mtr>
          <mtr>
            <mtd><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext>self-consistent</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd>
            <mtd><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Σ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mtd>
          </mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_&#123;\mathrm&#123;used&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;,\varepsilon)=\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;\varepsilon-\varepsilon(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)-\varepsilon_0&#125;,\qquad G_&#123;\mathrm&#123;self\text&#123;-&#125;consistent&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;,\varepsilon)=\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;\varepsilon-\varepsilon(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)-\Sigma(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;,\varepsilon)&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The first line is a shifted free propagator with the causal pole prescription specified in the paper; the shift is tied to the chemical potential. Hedin then uses <math class="math-inline" aria-label="Sigma equals i G W"><semantics><mrow><mi>Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\mathrm&#123;i&#125;GW</annotation></semantics></math> and the bubble <math class="math-inline" aria-label="P equals minus i G G"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=-\mathrm&#123;i&#125;GG</annotation></semantics></math>, which produces the Lindhard or RPA dielectric screening. The second line would feed the energy-dependent self-energy back into the propagator. The paper explicitly declines that larger numerical enterprise. Self-consistency is therefore part of the formal method but not fully realized in the reported electron-gas calculation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The paper evaluates continuous momentum and frequency integrals, not finite crystal meshes. It reports reducing the first-order self-energy to a two-dimensional integral. The dynamic second-order term is eight-dimensional and difficult to reduce below seven dimensions. For a rough estimate, Hedin replaces the dynamic screened interaction by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="W of k zero"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, leaving a five-dimensional integral; only at zero momentum and zero frequency does it reduce to the double integral evaluated with a Thomas–Fermi dielectric form. These reductions are the computational realization of the diagrammatic approximation.
  </p>
  <dl class="source-map">
    <dt>Physical system</dt><dd>Three-dimensional homogeneous electron gas with a uniform compensating background; most calculations use <math class="math-inline" aria-label="r s from one to six"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\le r_s\le6</annotation></semantics></math>.</dd>
    <dt>Propagator and screening</dt><dd>Shifted free propagator, bubble polarization, and RPA screened interaction; full energy-dependent self-consistency is not performed.</dd>
    <dt>Calculated objects</dt><dd>Correlation and total energies, Coulomb and correlation holes, quasiparticle energies, the nonlocal self-energy, quasiparticle interactions, specific heat, and paramagnetic susceptibility.</dd>
    <dt>Numerical detail reported</dt><dd>Integral reductions, selected static-screening estimates, density range, tabulated values, and an estimated numerical accuracy of about <math class="math-inline" aria-label="plus or minus zero point zero zero zero five rydberg"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.0005</mn><mspace width="0.2em"/><mtext>Ry</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm0.0005\,\mathrm&#123;Ry&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> for the stated energy calculation.</dd>
    <dt>Not reported in the paper</dt><dd>Code, programming language, hardware, quadrature algorithm, grid sizes, executable inputs, or modern reproducibility artifacts.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Hedin, Secs. 6–10, especially Eqs. (41)–(43) and (78)–(80), pp. A803 and A808; the numerical-accuracy statement is on p. A808.</p>

  <h2>Read the results as a convergence diagnosis</h2>
  <p>
    The electron-gas calculation is not presented as a universal accuracy benchmark. Hedin uses several observables to ask whether the expansion in <math class="math-inline" aria-label="W"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is settling down, and the answer depends on the object.
  </p>
  <dl class="source-map">
    <dt>Figs. 3–4 · p. A805</dt><dd>Begin with the pair-correlation curves. The RPA pair distribution becomes negative at short range, which is unphysical, while radial weighting suppresses some of that local failure in an integrated quantity. The plots diagnose an approximation; they do not turn a negative pair probability into acceptable physics.</dd>
    <dt>Fig. 5 · p. A805</dt><dd>Read the ladder-bubble diagrams as the proposed infinite vertex sum behind Eq. (61), not as particle trajectories. The paper introduces this resummation to repair a response-sign problem, then warns that selecting this subset alone does not systematically improve the self-energy and propagator.</dd>
    <dt>Fig. 6 · p. A806</dt><dd>Compare the real-space screening factor from RPA, Thomas–Fermi, and Pines forms. The figure asks how the medium reshapes the Coulomb interaction; it does not validate every short-range correlation quantity.</dd>
    <dt>Figs. 7–8 · pp. A807–A808</dt><dd>Compare potential and total energy versus <math class="math-inline" aria-label="r sub s"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_s</annotation></semantics></math>. The upper- and lower-bound discussion is used to estimate the error of the RPA-based chemical potential and the first self-energy term. These curves concern the uniform electron gas, not a material equation of state.</dd>
    <dt>Figs. 9–10 · pp. A811–A813</dt><dd>Fig. 9 separates the free dispersion from self-energy corrections; Fig. 10 plots the self-energy as a nonlocal real-space kernel. Look for the weak momentum dependence near the Fermi surface and the importance of energy dependence. Neither plot is a Kohn–Sham band structure.</dd>
    <dt>Figs. 11–12 · pp. A816–A817</dt><dd>The quasiparticle interaction and specific-heat ratio test derivatives rather than the magnitude of a single quasiparticle energy. Their poorer apparent convergence supports the paper’s warning that the paramagnetic susceptibility is unreliable in the alkali-metal-density regime.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>
    This sequence carries the paper’s strongest methodological lesson: success for one observable does not validate every derivative, response, or interaction built from the same approximation. The first screened-interaction term gives a useful quasiparticle-energy magnitude, but second-order terms are needed for its momentum derivative, and the quasiparticle interaction converges more poorly still.
  </p>

  <h2>What the paper established</h2>
  <dl class="source-map">
    <dt>Formal result</dt><dd>A self-consistent Green-function framework organized as an expansion in the screened interaction, with coupled self-energy, polarization, screened-interaction, and vertex relations.</dd>
    <dt>First practical truncation</dt><dd>The first screened-interaction self-energy with bubble polarization—the approximation later called GW—and a static COHSEX interpretation.</dd>
    <dt>Reported numerical result</dt><dd>An electron-gas test showing different convergence quality for quasiparticle-energy magnitude, its momentum dependence, total energy, quasiparticle interaction, and susceptibility.</dd>
    <dt>Not established</dt><dd>A universally accurate GW variant, a universally reliable real-material band-gap prediction scheme, full self-consistent GW for the electron gas, or reliable susceptibility at all metallic densities.</dd>
    <dt>Modern boundary</dt><dd>Today “GW” must name its starting point, self-consistency, vertex treatment, frequency representation, basis, unoccupied-state space, screening model, and dimensional Coulomb boundary. Those choices are not specified by the two-letter label.</dd>
  </dl>

